      "t": "Виктория Сергеевна, подождите.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Victoria Sergeyevna, wait.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'Viktoriya' with 'wait'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Я не прощу себе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I'll never forgive myself.' The negative particle sits directly before the verb for 'will forgive' to negate the action.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Если не спрошу сейчас.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'If I don't ask you this right now.' Notice how 'if' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'now'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "У вас есть муж?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you have a husband?' To express possession, Russian uses the preposition 'by' plus the genitive pronoun followed by 'husband'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Или парень?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Or boyfriend?' the guest combines 'or' with 'boyfriend' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ни мужа, ни парня нет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I don't have a husband or a boyfriend.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'not a single' with 'no'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Я буду ждать новой встречи.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
